 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Volume Scattering Integration:** In volume rendering, the scattering integral is $\int phase \times L d\Omega$. There is no $\cos\theta$ term from the surface area projection (as in surface rendering). The PDF used for importance sampling must match the phase function exactly without additional geometric terms [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Degenerate Incident Direction:** When constructing the local coordinate system for sampling, ensure the auxiliary vector is not parallel to the incident direction to avoid numerical instability in cross products [S2, G, unofficial].
-*   **Spectral Sampling:** If using wavelength-dependent scattering coefficients, distance sampling in the volume should use the maximum $\sigma_s$ across all wavelengths to avoid bias. Per-wavelength transmittance weighting must then be applied [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Volume Scattering Integration:** In volume rendering, the scattering integral is $\int phase \times L d\Omega$ [S3, unofficial]. There is no $\cos\theta$ term from the surface area projection, so the PDF evaluation for the phase function must not include geometric cosine terms [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Degenerate Incident Direction:** When constructing the local coordinate system for sampling, ensure the auxiliary vector is not parallel to the incident direction to avoid numerical instability in cross products [G].
+*   **Spectral Sampling:** If using wavelength-dependent scattering coefficients, distance sampling in the volume should use the maximum $\sigma_s$ across all wavelengths to avoid bias [S3, unofficial]. Per-wavelength transmittance weighting must then be applied via MIS or spectral throughput tracking [S3, unofficial].
 
